@page "/Projects/Portfolio"

<div class="ProjectPage">
  <DefaultProjectHeading CurrentProjectURL="Projects/Portfolio" />
  <div class="ProjectDescription">
    <h3>Project description</h3>
    <span>
      I built a Blazor site to display projects I'm proud of including this portfolio itself.
      Because we only learned Unity and Unreal during my studies, web development was a real challenge: I wasn't
      familiar
      with basic web concepts such as how the DOM works or the difference between client-side and server-side routing.
    </span>
    <h3>How the start of the project went</h3>
    <span>
      At the beginning of the project I was advised by my one of my classmates to build my portfolio site with React.
      So with little JavaScript knowledge I started off with React.
      It was very daunting but I managed to get a basic portfolio site going;
      it would probably be last in a beauty contest though...
    </span>

    <span>
      After a break, I forgot most of what I had made, and when I read through the HTML/TypeScript mix I thought,
      "Surely something with more comprehensible syntax must exist." I had already heard of Blazor, so I decided to give
      it a try.
    </span>
    <h3>The start of the Blazor project</h3>
    <span>
      After transferring HTML and CSS from my React project, I had a basic Blazor site. I didn't need to learn a lot of
      new syntax; I just used the template code as a reference. The Razor syntax felt easier compared to React, and
      already knowing the layout of the elements really helped. The designs I made in Adobe XD reinforced that.
    </span>
    <div class="ImageGrid"><img class="first" src="/images/projects/portfoliowebsite/Projectlayout.png" />
      <img class="second" src="/images/projects/portfoliowebsite/Projectpreviews.png" />
    </div>
    <img src="/images/projects/portfoliowebsite/Landingpage.png" />
    <span>
      You might recognize some elements from my React site. Some from the current design and some didn't make the cut.
      Some more modern designs are also present. Like these:
    </span>
    <div class="ImageGrid">
      <img src="/images/projects/portfoliowebsite/AboutMeDesign.png" />
      <img src="/images/projects/portfoliowebsite/ProjectTimeLineDesign.png" />
    </div>

    <h3>The development process</h3>
    <span>
      What I enjoyed most about Blazor was the routing system and the built-in scoped CSS. What took me the most time,
      however, was figuring out what the wwwroot is and what a static page means. That seems silly in hindsight, but
      it's
      understandable given my background: I'd only used .NET in a game programming context before.
      I didn't expect to navigate to a folder on the server to retrieve HTML. Obviously that's not how a Blazor
      WebAssembly app works: a Blazor WASM app is downloaded to the client and runs locally. I'm glad I realized that
      early on.
    </span>

    <h3>Switching host</h3>
    <span>
      GitHub Pages doesn't support client-side navigation for Blazor WASM apps (it will always look for an HTML file
      at the URI path). Blazor doesn't use hash-based routing either, so switching hosting became necessary. I chose
      Azure Static Web Apps because it has a free tier and supports Blazor WASM apps out of the box. When I first
      pushed the site to Azure, I forgot to set a root fallback.
    </span>
    <CodeBlock CodeString=@staticwebconfig />
    <span>
      When I tried to push this staticwebapp.config.json to Azure however it just rejected it with some weird characters
      attached to my file. I had never seen those in my file and I surely didn't add them myself.
      Apparently Visual Studio was saving the file with BOM encoding which Azure didn't like.
      After resaving the file without BOM everything worked perfectly.
      Now only thing was left to do...
    </span>

    <h3>DNS configuration</h3>
    <span>
      Setting up a custom domain on Azure was actually pretty easy.
      As I already struggled with DNS settings before with GitHub I knew what a CNAME and TXT record were.
      Azure just had those ready for me to copy paste in my DNS provider.
      After setting up the CNAME record and verifying ownership through a TXT record my custom domain was live in no
      time (for DNS standards I mean).
      As only two SSL certificates are allowed on the free tier I set one of my domains on redirect. This required
      migrating it to Cloudflare as my previous DNS provider didn't support HTTPS -> HTTPS redirects.
      Now <a>https://noradegreeuw.nl</a> redirects to <a>https://noradegreeuw.dev</a> as I don't have a Dutch version
      set up yet.
    </span>
    <h3>Where I am now</h3>
    <span>I think I'll never be done with this site. I enjoy writing blogs for it (even though they are very time
      consuming).
      And since I've also become pretty comfortable with CSS and Razor in general maintaining a website has become
      pretty
      fun. Next on the menu is migrating all eligible data to MongoDB and hopefully learning backend properly in the
      process.</span>
  </div>
</div>

@code {
  string staticwebconfig = CodeBlockDictionary.CodeBlocks["staticwebconfig"];
  string PortfolioGrid = CodeBlockDictionary.CodeBlocks["PortfolioGrid"];
}